---
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
---

<Layout>
  <main
    class="flex flex-col items-center justify-center min-h-screen text-white font-mono px-6 py-16 text-center"
  >
    <h1 class="text-4xl md:text-6xl mb-8 animate-fade-in-slow">My Interests</h1>
    <!-- interest -->

    <p
      class="max-w-3xl text-base md:text-lg leading-relaxed animate-fade-in-slow opacity-80"
    >
      I am interested in how <strong class="text-indigo-300"
        >internal representations form, stabilize, and change over time</strong
      >. Specifically:
    </p>

    <!-- questions -->
    <div class="max-w-2xl w-full mt-5 animate-fade-in-slow opacity-80">
      <p
        class="bold italic space-y-1 text-xs md:text-sm leading-relaxed text-gray-500"
      >
        What happens to existing representations when a system incorporates new
        information? What distinguishes transient perturbations from
        "meaningful" representational change? Under what conditions does that
        change result in structural reorganization? And more importantly, how
        can these processes be formalized?
      </p>
    </div>
    <!-- approaches -->
    <p
      class="max-w-3xl mt-5 text-base md:text-lg leading-relaxed animate-fade-in-slow opacity-80"
    >
      I approach these questions through <strong class="text-indigo-300"
        >mathematical modeling and computational experiments</strong
      >, focusing on dynamical systems, associative memory, and learning
      dynamics.
    </p>

    <p
      class="max-w-3xl mt-8 text-base md:text-lg leading-relaxed animate-fade-in-slow opacity-80"
    >
      Memory becomes a natural domain when studying these processes because it
      requires systems to preserve, modify, and reconstruct existing
      representations in response to new information. Broadly speaking, I am
      interested in whether mathematical principles can help describe <strong
        class="text-indigo-300">representational change across systems</strong
      >.
    </p>
  </main>
</Layout>

<style>
  .animate-fade-in-slow {
    opacity: 0;
    transform: translateY(20px);
    animation: floatFade 2.2s ease forwards;
  }

  @keyframes floatFade {
    to {
      opacity: 0.8;
      transform: translateY(0);
    }
  }

  html,
  body {
    min-height: 100%;
    margin: 0;
    overflow-y: auto;
  }

  body::-webkit-scrollbar {
    display: none;
  }

  body {
    -ms-overflow-style: none;
    scrollbar-width: none;
  }
</style>
